# Every harness script that runs the core-agent binary pins its config
# with -c.
#
# The hazard (#1116/D3): config.Find walks UP from the process cwd and
# returns the FIRST .agents/ it meets. A .agents/ at the repo root —
# committed with a recipe, or just left behind by a local run — is
# therefore inherited by every unpinned invocation anywhere under the
# checkout. The scripts keep passing, against an agent nobody chose: a
# different model, a different permission mode, a tools.disable list
# that removes the tool under test.
#
# The pin has to be -c and not --agents-dir. loadConfig() reads
# config.json by walking up from cwd BEFORE resolveAgentsDir() applies
# the flag, so --agents-dir moves the skills, MCP servers and sessions
# and leaves the model, permissions and budgets behind. cmd/core-agent's
# splitTreeWarning() exists to warn about exactly that split.
#
# Modes:
#
#   verify-harness-config-pinned           # verify (this is what CI runs)
#   verify-harness-config-pinned --print   # dump every site and its pin
#
# --print is the half a violation scanner cannot do for itself: it says
# which sites the scanner still recognises. A site that silently stops
# being recognised — a script renames its binary variable, say — would
# otherwise drop out of the check with no output at all.
#
# Deliberately not a unit test: it reads dev/** as source, and the point
# is to fail the build before those scripts ever run.

set -euo pipefail
. "$(dirname "$0")/common.sh"
cd "$(repo_root)"

exec go run ./dev/harness-config-check "$@"
